<% if (it.hasAuth) { -%>

## How authentication works

This app is a **backend-for-frontend**. The browser never sees a token.

```
browser ──fetch──▶ /api/auth/*  (this app's route handlers)
                        │  reads the httpOnly cookie, adds Authorization
                        ▼
                   your API
```

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `POST /api/auth/register` | Create an account and start a session |
| `POST /api/auth/login` | Exchange credentials for a session |
| `POST /api/auth/refresh` | Rotate the session |
| `POST /api/auth/logout` | Revoke the session and clear the cookies |

Access and refresh tokens live in httpOnly, `sameSite=lax` cookies that
JavaScript cannot read. Server Components read them through
`src/lib/auth/session.ts`, which is marked `server-only` — importing it from a
client component is a build error, not a silent leak.

`src/middleware.ts` redirects signed-out visitors away from protected routes.
That is a convenience: every protected page checks the session again, and the
API remains the authority.
<% } -%>

## Project structure

```
src/
├── app/                  routes (App Router)
<% if (it.hasAuth) { -%>
│   ├── (auth)/           sign-in and sign-up
│   ├── (app)/            routes that require a session
│   └── api/auth/         route handlers that hold the tokens
<% } -%>
├── components/
│   ├── ui/               buttons, fields, modal, toast
│   └── states/           loading, empty and error states
└── lib/
    ├── env.ts            validated, server-only configuration
    ├── api/              the one place this app calls the API
<% if (it.hasAuth) { -%>
    └── auth/             session cookies and auth calls
<% } -%>
```
<% if (it.hasDocker) { -%>

## Docker

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

The image uses Next.js standalone output, runs as a non-root user, and takes all
configuration from the environment.
<% } -%>

